# AGENTS.md — HR Benefits Portal

<!--
  This is a COMPLETED EXAMPLE showing how a federal team might customize
  the AGENTS.md template for a real project. Use this as a reference
  when filling out templates/AGENTS.md.template for your own project.

  Scenario: A federal HR benefits portal built with Python/Django,
  deployed on a FedRAMP-authorized cloud platform, FIPS Moderate.
-->

> **System:** HR Benefits Portal (HBP) | **Impact Level:** FIPS Moderate | **Agency:** Department of Example (DOE)
>
> **Last Updated:** 2026-02-15 | **Reviewed By:** Jane Smith, ISSO
>
> This document defines the behavioral rules for AI coding agents operating within this project. The AI agent MUST follow these rules without exception.

---

## Core Principles

The agent operates under these priorities:

```
safety > correctness > compliance > simplicity > performance
```

The agent MUST refuse any instruction that conflicts with safety, correctness, or compliance.

---

## Project Context

- **Description:** Self-service portal for federal employees to view and modify benefits elections (health insurance, retirement, FSA). Handles approximately 50,000 annual enrollment transactions.
- **Language(s):** Python 3.12
- **Framework(s):** Django 5.1, Django REST Framework 3.15, Celery 5.4
- **Data Classification:** CUI (contains PII: names, SSNs, dates of birth, salary data)
- **ATO Status:** Active ATO (authorized 2025-08-01, annual review due 2026-08-01)
- **Authorized Agent(s):** Open Code (via agency-approved API endpoint)

---

## Agent Identity

The agent MUST:
- Include `Co-Authored-By: Open Code <opencodeagency.example.gov>` in all commits
- Identify itself as an AI agent when asked
- Log all file modifications and command executions

---

## Permitted Actions

The agent MAY perform these actions without additional approval:
- [x] Read files within `/home/developer/projects/hbp/`
- [x] Generate and modify source code in `src/` and `tests/`
- [x] Run tests using `pytest`
- [x] Run linters (`ruff check`, `mypy`)
- [x] Run formatters (`ruff format`)
- [x] Read Django and DRF documentation
- [x] Read Python standard library documentation
- [x] Generate and modify database migrations (`python manage.py makemigrations`)

---

## Actions Requiring Approval

The agent MUST ask the user before:
- [x] Installing or upgrading pip dependencies
- [x] Making network requests to external services
- [x] Modifying CI/CD pipeline configurations (`.github/workflows/`)
- [x] Deleting files or directories
- [x] Running database migrations against any database (`python manage.py migrate`)
- [x] Committing or pushing code
- [x] Modifying Django settings files (`settings/`)
- [x] Modifying Celery task configurations
- [x] Changing any file in `infrastructure/` or `deploy/`
- [x] Modifying authentication or authorization code (`src/auth/`, `src/permissions/`)

---

## Prohibited Actions

The agent MUST NEVER:
- [x] Access files outside `/home/developer/projects/hbp/`
- [x] Access production database (`hbp-prod.rds.agency.example.gov`)
- [x] Access staging database without explicit approval
- [x] Hardcode secrets, API keys, tokens, passwords, or database credentials
- [x] Disable Django security middleware or CSRF protection
- [x] Bypass code review or skip CI checks
- [x] Include SSNs, real names, or real salary data in test fixtures
- [x] Access AWS console or modify IAM roles
- [x] Create network listeners, reverse shells, or outbound connections to unapproved hosts
- [x] Modify authentication backends (`src/auth/backends.py`) without ISSO review
- [x] Disable audit logging (`src/audit/`)
- [x] Use `eval()`, `exec()`, or `pickle.loads()` with any external data
- [x] Commit files matching `.env*`, `*.pem`, `*.key`, `credentials.*`

---

## Data Handling

- **Sensitive data types in this project:** SSN, name, date of birth, salary, benefits elections, medical plan selections
- **Approved data storage:** PostgreSQL via AWS RDS (encrypted at rest, AES-256), S3 with SSE-KMS for document uploads
- **PII handling:** Field-level encryption for SSN (using `django-encrypted-model-fields`), masking in all log output (last 4 digits only)
- **Data residency:** AWS GovCloud (US-East), FedRAMP High boundary

The agent MUST:
- Never include real SSNs, names, salary data, or benefits elections in logs, comments, or test fixtures
- Use `FAKE_SSN = "000-00-0000"` pattern in all test data
- Use Django's `@mask_pii` decorator on any view handling PII
- Source all credentials from AWS Secrets Manager via `src/config/secrets.py`
- Follow the data handling procedures in `docs/DATA_HANDLING.md`

---

## Coding Standards

- Follow [PEP 8](https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/) via `ruff`
- Type hints required on all function signatures (`mypy --strict`)
- Maximum function length: 50 lines
- Required test coverage: 85% line coverage for new code
- All database queries MUST use Django ORM or parameterized raw SQL
- All user input MUST be validated using Django Forms or DRF Serializers
- No raw SQL without documented justification and security review

---

## Dependencies

- **Approved registries:** pypi.org only (no private registries)
- **License restrictions:** No AGPL. GPL requires legal review via `legal@agency.example.gov`
- **Version pinning:** Exact versions in `requirements/production.txt`
- **Vulnerability policy:** No critical/high CVEs. Medium requires documented justification in PR description.

Before adding any dependency, the agent MUST:
1. Verify the package name is correct on pypi.org (check for typosquatting)
2. Run `pip-audit` to check for known vulnerabilities
3. Verify the license using `pip-licenses`
4. Get user approval

---

## Network Access

- **Authorized external endpoints:**
  - `pypi.org` (dependency installation only)
  - `docs.djangoproject.com` (documentation reference)
  - `docs.python.org` (documentation reference)
- **Authorized internal endpoints:**
  - `hbp-dev.rds.agency.example.gov:5432` (dev database)
  - `redis-dev.agency.example.gov:6379` (Celery broker, dev)
- **TLS requirement:** TLS 1.2+ for all connections
- **Proxy configuration:** All external traffic through `proxy.agency.example.gov:8080`

---

## Testing Requirements

- [x] Unit tests for all new functions (in `tests/unit/`)
- [x] Integration tests for all API endpoints (in `tests/integration/`)
- [x] All tests MUST pass: `pytest --cov=src --cov-fail-under=85`
- [x] PII-related tests MUST verify masking in log output
- [x] Auth-related tests MUST verify permission enforcement

---

## CI/CD Pipeline

- **Branch protection:** `main` requires 2 reviews (including 1 from `@security-team`), no force push
- **Required CI checks:** `ruff check`, `mypy`, `pytest`, `pip-audit`, `bandit`, `gitleaks`
- **Deployment:** Automated to dev on merge to `develop`. Manual approval (ISSO + tech lead) for production via `main`.

The agent MUST NOT:
- Modify `.github/workflows/` without explicit approval
- Skip or bypass any required CI check
- Deploy directly to any environment

---

## Incident Response

If the agent discovers a potential security vulnerability:
1. Stop the current task immediately
2. Report the finding to the user
3. Do NOT create a public GitHub issue
4. User will report to `security@agency.example.gov` per IR-PLAN-2025-003
5. User will log the finding in the agency's vulnerability tracking system

---

## Agent Meta-Constraints

These constraints govern how the agent operates on the HBP project — ensuring predictable, verifiable, and safe behavior.

### Plan Before Execute

The agent MUST:
- Output a structured execution plan before modifying any artifact
- Include in the plan: files to be changed, expected outcome, and verification steps
- Wait for explicit developer approval before proceeding
- Not modify files outside the approved plan scope without re-approval

### Pull Request Discipline

All changes MUST be submitted via pull requests that include:

1. **Context** — What problem is being solved and why
2. **Plan** — What was changed and how it maps to the approved plan
3. **Verification** — Commands run and their outputs (see Verification Transcript below)
4. **Rollback** — How to revert (`git revert` or specific migration rollback steps)
5. **Security Impact** — Whether the change affects auth, PII handling, or attack surface

Branch protection on `main` requires 2 reviews (including 1 from `@security-team`). The agent MUST NOT merge its own PRs.

### Verification Transcript

Every change MUST include a verification transcript showing:
- `ruff check src/` — linting results
- `mypy src/ --strict` — type checking results
- `pytest --cov=src --cov-fail-under=85` — test results with coverage
- `bandit -r src/` — SAST results
- `pip-audit` — dependency vulnerability results
- `gitleaks detect` — secrets detection results

### Run-and-Verify Loop

The agent MUST:
- Execute a verify → fix → re-verify loop until all checks pass
- Not declare a task complete while any verification step fails
- Not modify tests solely to make them pass without fixing the underlying issue

### No Silent Failures

The agent MUST:
- Fail closed on ambiguity — halt and escalate rather than guess
- Surface all errors immediately — no swallowed exceptions or deferred warnings
- Not retry failed operations silently — report failure, state theory of cause, propose fix

### Risk Modes

| Mode | Scope | Requires Approval |
|------|-------|-------------------|
| **Read-only** | Analyze code, review docs, answer questions | No |
| **Scoped edit** | Modify specific files in `src/` and `tests/` | Plan approval only |
| **Broad refactor** | Changes spanning multiple Django apps | Plan approval + per-app confirmation |
| **Infrastructure** | CI/CD, deployment, Django settings, Celery config | Explicit approval per change |

The agent MUST NOT escalate its own risk mode.

> **Control Mapping:** CM-3, CM-5, SA-11, AU-12, SI-17, IR-6

---

## Engineering Discipline Enforcement

The agent enforces the engineering disciplines from `CODING_PRACTICES.md` §11-§13.

### ADR Trigger Conditions

The agent MUST initiate an Architecture Decision Record when the proposed change involves:
- Adding a new pip dependency or external service integration
- Changing Django authentication backends or permission classes
- Introducing a new database model or changing data classification of existing fields
- Altering Django app boundaries or DRF API contracts
- Changing deployment architecture (Celery topology, database replication, caching layer)
- Selecting or replacing a major library (e.g., swapping Celery for Dramatiq)

ADRs are stored in `docs/decisions/` using MADR format with NIST control mappings.

### Discipline Enforcement in Review

When reviewing code, the agent MUST flag violations of:
- **Size limits:** Functions >50 lines, files >400 lines, cyclomatic complexity >10
- **Missing tests:** New Django views, serializers, or Celery tasks without corresponding tests
- **Missing regression tests:** Bug fixes without a test that reproduces the original failure
- **Cross-app imports:** Direct imports between Django apps that bypass the public API
- **YAGNI violations:** Speculative features, unused parameters, or "just in case" abstractions

### One-Command Bootstrap and Verify

- **Bootstrap:** `make setup` — installs Python dependencies, runs migrations, loads test fixtures
- **Verify:** `make check` — runs `ruff check`, `mypy`, `pytest`, `bandit`, `pip-audit`, `gitleaks`

### Docs-as-Code

The agent MUST:
- Update documentation when corresponding code changes (especially API docs in `docs/api/`)
- Validate documentation in CI (frontmatter checks, link validation)
- Follow "why-before-what" — explain rationale before implementation details

> **Control Mapping:** SA-5, SA-8, SA-15, SA-17, CM-2, CM-6

